{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1356910-a-small-town-on-the-ghorkon-white-elephant-for-unkowndarklord","id":1356910,"title":"A Small Town on The Ghorkon [White Elephant for UnkownDarklord]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-12-25T02:25:57-08:00","id":1356910,"title":"A Small Town on The Ghorkon [White Elephant for UnkownDarklord]","content":"A place in the taiga, not far from Whitewall, but old and secret.<br /> <br /> There is a small settlement in the taiga, not far from a small river called Ghorkon. It pays homage to White Wall in the form of cattle, but has little other contact with the city. Occasionally, one of the town\u2019s doctors or surgeons comes to the town to learn the trades they can. The town has grown from a village to a town by means of the Travelers Road and its own trade, but never very big. But often the village strives to be left alone. For most of the year, the people of the village live a nomadic life, returning to the site only in winter. Only the <i>menku</i> priest-butchers of the people and the attendant worm folk reside here permanently. A few other inhabitants live here, mostly outlaws and survivalists seeking safety in numbers.<br /> A number of strange buildings stand apart from the villages small tents, the yurts, and the huts\u2014the great Shrine of the Hundred Headed Herd for instance, which seems to be a hollowed out hill. All surgery and butchery is done within. Great Aurochs are examined in the bottom most depths, auguries among the primeval bull gods of the region, as well as other spirits of the village. The practices here are a closely guarded secret, by violence if necessary. The butchery here helps presever the meat for months, making a good export or rations on the kins long travels.<br /> According to the <i>menkhu</i> and the worm-folk in the area, it is the sacred nature of this site that creates the strange plants in the region. That and the ritual appeasement of the evils of the world, the blood sport in the great ring, where no weapons are permitted. The saturation of sacrificial blood, or strange lay lines, either produces the twyrine. These herbs, when properly prepared, help stave off illness and serve as a wonderful alcoholic beverage. Only the worm folk know the secret of growing them properly, however. Only they and menku are allowed to harvest the plants, as piercing the earth is seen as priest and butcher work (burials are sky burials or conducted by a priest).<br /> The town\u2019s other oddity is it\u2019s only major family of non-native inhabitants, the Canes, a group of occult scholars and thautmurgists who arrived in town a century ago. The family has relatives elsewhere in the North, including Whitewall, but its eldest members live in the small town by the Ghorkon. The four or five generation of Canes have become key figures among the other non-native groups, often serving as intermediaries. Their head, Symun, was respected by both kin and outsider as a man of character and considerable charisma. Especially regarding events about two decades prior.<br /> During that time omens began to appear. The fish in the river began washing ashore, scaleless. Eggs began to go foul, chickens missing in the night. And then the plague came. A strange plague, that slowly turned one\u2019s blood to something more like dirt, causing mass organ failure. It seemed to change it\u2019s patterns daily, striking this way and that, airborne one day, in the water the next. The menkhu priests and the shamans of the town were devastated as were the common folk, and the town shrank considerably. The nomadic cattle drivers felt the plagues grip swell in them, and even many of the cattle were slain. The town survived, with the help of a particularly skilled menkhu named Burakh and a man of some occult education named Symun. Rumors among the town claim that Symun, a man of such age that he has outlasted his great grandchildren, wrestled the diseases itself with his bare hands and trapped it with strange magics in a once pale white obelisk outside town. Such an obelisk does stand, now stained blood red. It is said that it and other standing stones are also sacred places to the Kin, part of warding off the Fae folk that lurk in the nearby area.<br /> Tales of all these strange happenings have left the town in a curious place. It is regarded with some distance by others\u2014a place to some degree cursed, but at the same time intriguing for what might be beneath those secrets. Those aware of the plague, and perhaps fearful of such a thing entering the Travelers Road and flowing down and across the north, have become aware of disquieting occurrence\u2014the death of both Symon and Burakh in the last year.<br /> Scaleless fish have begun washing ashore again. Symon\u2019s relatives in Whitewall have heard word that he has perished, and that some of his greatest projects have been left unfinished. His brother in town believes it a murder, by his one time ally Burakh to prevent whatever his grand design was.<br /> <br /> <b>What\u2019s Going On Really</b><br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> As this is for a detective game in Whitewall, perhaps some elaboration is necessary. Not all the secrets, of course. Not everything behind the curtains is set in stone, and surely somethings are best left unsaid and unlearned. But here is something true.<br /> The plague is returning. Soon. In two weeks, it will have overtaken most of the town again\u2014Symun\u2019s imprisonment of the Disease God behind it held it back but he lacked the might or perhaps interest to slay it outright. Why the village has been condemned by the God of the Wasting Plagues is unsure. The god was not a mere Disease God, but one of rank in the courts of sores and pox. His attendants have found him and now plan to ruin the town for the imprisonment. Whether they killed the sorcerer Symun (for he was an initiated into those mysteries) or not is not to be disclosed. But certainly they benefit. The original plague is a supernatural plague. The God of the Wasting Plague has the unique property of changing the diseases method of spread. It takes the form of a great cloud of buzzing black shapes, topped with a local animal skull. It\u2019s courtiers often take the form of strange pale clay figures.<br /> Symun\u2019s relatives are concerned about finding the cause of Symun\u2019s death, and the result of his sorcerous workings that now lay unfinished\u2014great occult energies bent into a terrible tower. Perhaps Symun planned something for the diseases god here\u2014the interior is an intoxicating place, especially for children, and most local spirits shun the place. However, it is unfinished. It is slowly decaying into something worse.<br /> The <i>menku</i> Burakh made considerable progress towards a cure for the illness, but on his own he will perish as well\u2014the plague spirits that attended that great and bloated god have marked him for a hundred illnesses. Perhaps among his and Symon\u2019s things a solution can be found\u2014or at least, how to combat the stirring agent. His only son is on his own way back from studying in Whitewall under a Realm educated doctor.<br /> If the plague is unleashed, there is a danger it will reach tendrils up to the Travelers Road and ride it across the North. The exact details of its virulence and symptoms are not to be disclosed.<br /> The great structure predates the inhabitants to at least the First Age. It\u2019s apparent natural construction has to do the sorcerous workings used to build the place\u2014whether always as a butcher house or perhaps for another purpose is not to be disclosed. It may even date back further\u2014perhaps never meant for mortal hands. The area is an Earth demense, and if one digs more than three inches deep, dark red liquid pools out. Possibly blood. This is not to be disclosed.<br /> The worm folk are a simple mystery\u2014they are beast men, blessed by either a god or a lunar in time immemorial. Their ritual practices form a local thautmurgy of planting blood to create the necessary plants\u2014this works best in the area around the Maw, but perhaps would work elsewhere.<br /> The plants themselves work well as Natural Wonders. </div></div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</